### How to Use This Code:

1.  **Save the code:**
    *   Open a plain text editor (like Notepad on Windows, TextEdit on Mac, or VS Code).
    *   Copy and paste the entire code block above into the editor.
    *   Save the file with an `.html` extension (e.g., `index.html`, `mypage.html`). Make sure you save it as "All Files" or "Plain Text" to avoid adding `.txt` at the end.

2.  **Open in a browser:**
    *   Locate the saved `.html` file on your computer.
    *   Double-click the file, and it should open in your default web browser.

### Explanation of the Code:

*   `<!DOCTYPE html>`: Declares the document type and version of HTML.
*   `<html lang="en">`: The root element of an HTML page. `lang="en"` specifies the language as English.
*   `<head>`: Contains meta-information about the HTML document, such as the character set, viewport settings, and the page title.
    *   `<meta charset="UTF-8">`: Specifies the character encoding for the document, which is important for displaying characters correctly.
    *   `<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">`: Configures the viewport for responsive design, ensuring the page scales well on different devices.
    *   `<title>My Simple Webpage</title>`: Sets the title that appears in the browser tab or window's title bar.
    *   `<style>`: This block contains CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) to style the appearance of the HTML elements.
*   `<body>`: Contains the actual content of the HTML document that will be displayed in the browser.
    *   `<header>`: Represents introductory content, typically a group of introductory or navigational aids.
        *   `<h1>Welcome to My Simple Webpage</h1>`: The main heading of the page.
    *   `<main>`: Represents the dominant content of the `<body>` of a document.
        *   `<section>`: Defines a thematic grouping of content, typically with a heading.
            *   `<h2>About This Page</h2>`: A secondary heading.
            *   `<p>...</p>`: Defines a paragraph of text.
            *   `<ul>`: Defines an unordered list (bullet points).
            *   `<li>`: Defines a list item within a `<ul>` or `<ol>`.
            *   `<a href="..." target="_blank">...</a>`: Defines a hyperlink.
                *   `href="..."`: Specifies the URL the link goes to.
                *   `target="_blank"`: Opens the link in a new tab.
    *   `<footer>`: Represents a footer for its nearest sectioning content or the root element (`<html>`).
        *   `<p>&copy; 2023 My Website. All rights reserved.</p>`: A copyright notice. `&copy;` is an HTML entity for the copyright symbol.

